# Answer 15 · Masculinity and Femininity Defined; Where Traditional Gender Roles Come From (CSS 2023)
@py question 2023-4

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Define both terms simply (plural, relational), then give your opinion on where roles come from — six sources, each with evidence.
@ask ① DEFINE masculinity and femininity
@ask ② IN YOUR OPINION, where do traditional gender roles come from?
(1) Introduction: roles feel natural; Beauvoir says women are made; thesis — biological start, social history
(2) Masculinity: what society expects of men; Connell's four types
(3) Femininity: what society expects of women; emphasised femininity; sharif aurat
(4) Source 1, biology: pregnancy and strength gave a starting point only
(5) Source 2, the economy: plough farming and the breadwinner wage fixed roles
(6) Source 3, property: men controlled women to pass land to sons (Engels)
(7) Source 4, religion and culture: interpretations and honour codes
(8) Source 5, law and colonial rule: family laws; Victorian ideals; 1980s slogans
(9) Source 6, upbringing and media: family, school, TV
(10) How the sources work together: each supports the others
(11) Pakistan today: roles changing, but only 24.4% of women in the labour force
(12) Conclusion: roles were built, so they can be rebuilt
:::

## Decoding the question
**"Define"** asks for clear definitions of two terms — the best show that each is **plural, relational and changeable**. **"In your opinion"** invites your own view, but it still needs evidence. **"Where do traditional gender roles come from?"** is a question about **causes**: give several sources, explain how each works, and reach a judgement.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Roles that feel natural
In most societies, including Pakistan's, it seems natural that men should **earn and lead** while women should **care and keep the home**.

#### ii) Beauvoir's challenge
**Simone de Beauvoir** warned in ==1949== that "**one is not born, but rather becomes, a woman**". Masculinity and femininity are the ideals through which people "become" men and women.

#### iii) My view
In my opinion, traditional roles have a **biological starting point but a social history**. The economy, property, religion, law and upbringing turned small biological differences into **rigid, unequal rules** — which is why the rules can change.

### 2. Masculinity defined

::: define Masculinity | what society expects of men
The behaviour, qualities, roles and ideals a society links with **being a man** — usually strength, courage, hiding emotion, providing for the family and authority over dependants.
-> Masculinity is plural (many forms), relational (defined as "not feminine") and ranked.
:::

#### Connell's four masculinities
**Raewyn Connell** (*Masculinities*, ==1995==) showed that masculinities are **ranked**:
- **Hegemonic** — the **most honoured** way of being a man, justifying men's power;
- **Complicit** — most men, who do not match the ideal but gain the "**patriarchal dividend**";
- **Marginalised** — men placed lower by class or ethnicity;
- **Subordinated** — men looked down on as "unmanly".

*Pakistan:* the ***ghairat-mand*** man who protects and controls the family's women is the honoured ideal in many communities.

### 3. Femininity defined

::: define Femininity | what society expects of women
The behaviour, qualities, roles and ideals a society links with **being a woman** — usually modesty, gentleness, care, beauty, obedience and sacrifice for the family.
-> Femininity is usually defined in relation to — and in the service of — the dominant masculinity.
:::

Connell called the honoured form **emphasised femininity** — femininity built around **pleasing men**. In Pakistan the ideal of the ***sharif aurat*** (respectable woman) who guards the family's ***izzat*** (honour) plays this role; women who work late, speak out or choose their own husbands may be condemned.

### 4. Source one: biology and childbirth

**Pregnancy and breastfeeding** tied women to young children for long periods, and men's greater average **upper-body strength** shaped some tasks. Biology explains why **some division of work first appeared**. But it cannot explain why that division became a **hierarchy**, why it differs so much between societies, or why it lasts when strength no longer matters for most jobs.

### 5. Source two: the economy

#### i) The plough
**Ester Boserup** (==1970==) noticed that societies which farmed with the heavy **plough** developed sharper gender roles than those using the hoe. The economists **Alesina, Giuliano and Nunn** (==2013==) found that people whose ancestors used the plough **still hold less equal views** today.

#### ii) The factory
Industry later separated **home from work** and created the ideal of the **male breadwinner** and the dependent housewife.

### 6. Source three: property and inheritance

**Friedrich Engels** (*The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State*, ==1884==) argued that when men gathered property, they wanted to pass it to **their own sons**, so they came to control women's sexuality and keep them at home — "**the world historical defeat of the female sex**". In South Asia, control of **land** and the denial of **daughters' inheritance** show how property and gender roles remain linked.

### 7. Source four: religion and culture

Religious interpretations, customs and **honour codes** give roles to men and women and make them feel sacred. These interpretations are **debated within every tradition** — Muslim scholars such as **Amina Wadud** and **Fatima Mernissi** argue that some patriarchal readings reflect culture more than revelation — but in daily life they are a powerful source of "traditional" roles.

### 8. Source five: law, the state and colonial rule

#### i) Law
Laws on marriage, guardianship, inheritance and nationality **write roles into the state**. For example, laws that name the father as the natural **guardian** of children assume that men hold authority in the family, even where mothers do all the caring.

#### ii) Colonial rule and recent "tradition"
**British rule** brought Victorian ideals of women's place in the home and hardened customary law. In Pakistan, ***chador aur chaar-diwari*** ("the veil and the four walls") was promoted as an ideal in the **1980s** — a reminder that "tradition" is sometimes recent policy.

### 9. Source six: upbringing and media

#### i) How children learn
**Social learning theory** (**Albert Bandura**) and **gender schema theory** (**Sandra Bem**, ==1981==) explain how children absorb roles through **reward, copying and mental maps**.

#### ii) Examples
Families give boys **freedom** and girls **housework**; textbooks show mothers cooking and fathers in offices; television dramas reward the **self-sacrificing wife**; adverts sell detergents to women and cars to men.

### 10. How the sources work together

::: fig cycle | How the six sources of traditional roles reinforce each other.
@centre Traditional gender roles
Biology: a small starting difference
Economy: turns it into a division of work
Religion and culture: make it feel sacred
Law and state: write it into rules
Upbringing and media: teach it to children
:::

The sources **support each other**. A biological difference is **exaggerated** by the economy, **justified** by culture, **written into law** and then **taught to children** as "the way things are". This chain is why traditional roles feel so natural — and why changing them needs action on **several fronts at once**. Changing only one link — for example passing a law — is weakened if the others stay the same.

### 11. Pakistan today

The strongest evidence that roles are social is that they **change**. Women's literacy has risen to about ==53%== (Census 2023); women serve as **Supreme Court judges**, **fighter pilots** and, since ==2024==, a provincial **chief minister**; more fathers share childcare. Yet only ==24.4%== of working-age women are in the labour force (LFS 2024–25), showing how strong traditional roles remain. A fair critic would add that many people **freely choose** and value caring roles; the problem is not the roles themselves but their **compulsory, unequal and unpaid** character.

### 12. Conclusion
Masculinity and femininity are the ideals through which a society makes men and women — **plural, ranked and changeable**. In my opinion, traditional roles come from a **combination of sources**: biology gave a starting point, but the economy, property, religion and culture, law and the state, and daily upbringing built it into a **rigid hierarchy** and passed it on. Because the roles were **built**, they can be **rebuilt** — and Pakistan's own recent history shows that they already are being.

## Why this answer scores
- Both terms are defined **simply and precisely**, with **Connell's types** and Pakistani ideals.
- "In your opinion" is answered with a **clear thesis**, stated at the start and the end.
- **Six sources**, each with its own heading and evidence (Boserup, Alesina et al., Engels, Bandura, Bem).
- A **cycle figure** shows how the sources reinforce each other.
- Balance: biology is **acknowledged**, not dismissed, and freely chosen roles are respected.
